{"type":"assistant.message","data":{"messageId":"8c579765-9b2d-4f5a-9976-1d6c5ade4e22","content":"No — don't transfer that folder. `C:\\Program Files\\Blender Foundation` contains the Windows build of Blender (Windows `.exe`/`.dll` binaries), which won't run on Linux. Just install Blender natively on Linux instead:\n\n- **Official**: download the Linux build from blender.org, extract, and run.\n- **Package manager**: e.g. `sudo apt install blender` (Ubuntu/Debian) or `sudo dnf install blender` (Fedora).\n- **Flatpak/Snap**: `flatpak install flathub org.blender.Blender`.\n\nWhat you *may* want to carry over is your **user data** (preferences, add-ons, startup file), not the program itself. On Windows that lives in:\n\n```\nC:\\Users\\<you>\\AppData\\Roaming\\Blender Foundation\\Blender\\<version>\\\n```\n\nThe equivalent location on Linux is:\n\n```\n~/.config/blender/<version>/\n```\n\nInside that you'll find `config/` (preferences, keymaps, startup.blend) and `scripts/addons/` (custom add-ons). Copy those subfolders over if you want to keep your setup. Note that add-ons with compiled/native components should be reinstalled from their Linux versions rather than copied.\n\nYour actual project files (`.blend`) are fully cross-platform — just copy them normally.","toolRequests":[],"reasoningText":"The user's asking whether they can just copy their Windows Blender folder over to Linux, but that won't work since Windows executables don't run on Linux. They'll need to install the Linux version separately, though they can transfer their user configuration, addons, and project files if they want to keep their setup intact."},"id":"e4af1f4d-9068-445e-8629-dbcda385e647","timestamp":"2026-07-06T13:21:38.367Z","parentId":"b4978343-2b98-434a-a724-ffd24aa7bf46"}
